We adopt a factorial ablation across four factors: $\dt$-embedding mode (trained / frozen / none), temporal subsampling (on / off), imagination horizon ($H{=}15$ / $H{=}8$), and imagination step size ($\dt{=}1$, $2$, $4$). The design (12 conditions, Table~\ref{tab:factorial_design} in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}) isolates trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as orthogonal factors. Key configurations use 4 seeds; following \citet{agarwal2021precipice}, we report per-seed trajectories.

\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\paragraph{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$).}
We define $\nve_t = (v/e)_{\text{config},t} / (v/e)_{\text{baseline},t}$, where $v/e$ is the critic's mean imagined value divided by mean evaluation return, and the baseline is the highest-performing seed (exp\_001, seed~1, 505K steps). We restrict to a \textbf{35K+ window} to avoid early-training inflation. The $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold achieves 26/29 accuracy (AUROC${=}0.953$, in-sample; LOEO cross-validated, mean AUROC${=}0.981$, Appendix~Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}).

\subsection{Selective Gradient Stopping}
\label{sec:sgs_method}

To identify which gradient pathway through $e_{\Delta t}$ drives CIVO, we introduce \textit{selective gradient stopping} (SGS): $e_{\Delta t}$ is detached via $\operatorname{sg}(\cdot)$ before selected prediction heads during the backward pass, preventing those loss gradients from updating $e_{\Delta t}$ while preserving its forward-pass contribution. We test three variants: reward-only (exp\_016e), full-pathway (exp\_018, all non-KL gradients blocked), and obs-decoder-only (exp\_025/026). Gradient decomposition using $\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_i$ quantifies each pathway's contribution (Appendix~\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}).

\subsection{Forward-Pass Interventions}
\label{sec:forward_pass_method}

We test two forward-pass interventions as representation- and output-level probes: \textbf{TCN} adds Gaussian noise $\mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ to $e_{\Delta t}$ during imagination ($\sigma \in \{0.5, 0.2\}$, three environments); \textbf{RAVE} clips imagined values exceeding an EMA anchor ($c \in \{2.0, 1.5\}$).

\subsection{Dual-Critic Mechanism Ablation}
\label{sec:dual_critic_method}

To test whether standard overestimation countermeasures address CIVO, we conduct a $2{\times}2$ factorial: \{single, dual\} $\times$ \{narrow (${\sim}1.2$M), wide (${\sim}2.4$M)\} critic parameters. The dual-critic variant follows TD3 \citep{fujimoto2018td3}: two independent value heads trained on shared RSSM features, with the actor optimizing $\min(v_1, v_2)$. The wide variant doubles hidden dimensions to control for capacity independently of the min operation.

\subsection{Environment and Training Details}
\label{sec:env_details}

All experiments use Walker Walk and Cheetah Run from the DeepMind Control Suite (DMC; \citealt{tassa2018dmcontrol}) with proprioceptive observations, chosen for their contrasting dynamics (6-joint biped vs.\ 2-joint runner). Walker Walk serves as the primary environment with the full factorial design; Cheetah Run serves as a severity-dependent generalization probe: CIVO manifests at $8$--$70\times$ NVE on Cheetah versus $2$--$4\times$ on Walker, testing whether interventions transfer under amplified severity rather than replicating the full factorial. The baseline runs for 500K steps (peak return 970.4); treatments run for 105K steps, sufficient to observe collapse onset. All hyperparameters follow the DreamerV3 defaults \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} without task-specific tuning ($d{=}32$ temporal embedding; full hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{sec:hyperparameters}). Training uses a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (${\sim}80$ GPU-hours total). The codebase extends the official DreamerV3 implementation\footnote{\url{https://github.com/danijar/dreamerv3}, accessed March 2026.} with the variable-rate modules described in \S\ref{sec:variable_rate}.

The three false positives (FPR${=}0.18$) are mechanistically distinct from CIVO: (1)~RAVE anchor pollution (exp\_028, the EMA anchor becomes inflated, producing overestimation through a different pathway); (2)~obs-decoder SGS-induced value distortion (exp\_025 seed~2, blocking observation decoder gradients damages the RSSM latent space); (3)~IRD eval-driven collapse (exp\_008e\_v3 seed~2, actor entropy \emph{increases} with evaluation collapse, mechanistically distinct from CIVO's entropy \emph{decrease}). All three produce NVE $> 1.5\times$ through non-CIVO mechanisms, indicating that the NVE diagnostic detects value overestimation broadly rather than CIVO specifically---a feature rather than a limitation for practitioners monitoring deployment health. The closest sub-threshold case (008f seed~4, NVE${=}1.41\times$) lacks $\dt$-conditioning entirely ($d{=}0$); its elevated NVE reflects transient value noise that self-corrects without the positive feedback loop characteristic of CIVO. The closest above-threshold positive (008d seed~3, NVE${=}1.53\times$) exhibits the full CIVO causal chain: trained $\dt$-embedding, entropy decrease, and evaluation collapse to 14\% of baseline.

\subsection{NVE Normalization Robustness}
\label{sec:nve_robustness}

To assess sensitivity to baseline seed choice, we extend the single-seed baseline (seed~1, eval 915.7 at 105K) to three additional seeds trained to 505K steps (Table~\ref{tab:baseline_seeds}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Walker Walk baseline seeds at 105K and 505K steps.}
\label{tab:baseline_seeds}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Eval@105K} & \textbf{Eval@505K} & \textbf{ve@65K} & \textbf{ve@105K} \\
\midrule
s1 & 915.7 & 970.4 & 0.563 & 0.656 \\
s2 & 443.5 & 945.7 & 0.898 & 0.742 \\
s3 & 869.0 & 947.7 & --- & 0.664 \\
s4 & 922.8 & 938.9 & --- & 0.624 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Seed~2 exhibits slow convergence (eval 443.5 at 105K, recovering to 945.7 by 512K), confirming non-pathological slow convergence rather than seed failure. Its elevated $v/e$ ratio at 65K (0.898 vs.\ 0.563 for seed~1) reflects the low evaluation denominator rather than pathological value overestimation. Using seed~2 as the sole normalization reference systematically deflates NVE values, causing two marginal CIVO-positive configurations (008d seed~3, peak NVE $1.53\times$; 009b seed~2, peak NVE $1.62\times$) to fall below the $1.5\times$ threshold. Core CIVO cases (peak NVE $> 3\times$) remain correctly classified under all baseline seeds.

Single-seed normalization (s1) yields AUROC $= 0.933$ and accuracy 7/8 (87.5\%) on the Walker subset. Four-seed mean normalization yields AUROC $= 0.867$ and accuracy 6/8 (75.0\%): the boundary case exp\_009b flips from $\nve = 1.62\times$ (s1) to $1.23\times$ (4-seed mean), because its peak occurs at 45K when the s1 $v/e$ ratio (0.331) is well below the 4-seed mean (0.436). All high-NVE configurations remain correctly classified (006: $4.84\times \to 4.52\times$; 008d: $4.12\times \to 3.54\times$). The $1.5\times$ threshold thus correctly identifies all strong CIVO cases regardless of baseline choice; sensitivity is confined to configurations within 10\% of the threshold.

\paragraph{Normalization conventions.} Walker NVE is normalized against the 4-seed mean baseline (seeds~1--4, each run to 505K+ steps). Cheetah NVE is normalized against a 5-seed mean baseline (seeds~0--4, each run to ${\sim}$210K steps). The differing seed counts reflect available baseline data; both provide sufficient coverage of the respective experimental windows (Walker: 210K analysis window; Cheetah: 105K analysis window).

\paragraph{Limitations.} With $n = 10$ experiments, Clopper-Pearson confidence intervals remain wide (e.g., 90.0\% accuracy has 95\% CI [55.5\%, 99.7\%]). These results should be interpreted as a diagnostic proof-of-concept rather than a validated clinical threshold. Replication across additional environments and seeds is needed to establish tighter bounds. Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2_full} reports the original $n{=}16$ evaluation set spanning both environments. The expanded analysis ($n{=}29$, including SGS variants, RAVE, and TCN configurations) yields AUROC${=}0.953$ (Figure~\ref{fig:nve_roc}, in-sample, post-hoc). The original $n{=}16$ core analysis yields AUROC${=}0.969$; the expanded $n{=}29$ set includes SGS, RAVE, TCN, and dual-critic configurations. Cross-architecture validation is deferred to future work.

\subsection{Leave-One-Environment-Out Cross-Validation}
\label{sec:nve_loeo}

To assess whether $\theta{=}1.5\times$ generalizes beyond the in-sample evaluation, we perform leave-one-environment-out (LOEO) cross-validation across Walker ($n{=}13$), Cheetah ($n{=}5$), and PickCube ($n{=}2$). For each fold, we hold out all experiments from one environment, select the optimal accuracy-maximizing threshold range on the remaining training set, and evaluate the fixed $\theta{=}1.5\times$ on the held-out environment.

